
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Recorded: shared app state, so we're building a real session module with a lifecycle.

  Second question — this is what "persist" actually means, and it's the decision with a security dimension.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how long the userId outlives the login that produced it. There's no logout anywhere in this app today, so
  whatever I pick [3mis[0m the only thing that ever clears the value.

  - [1mPer-tab ([38;5;153msessionStorage[39m)[0m — survives reloads and navigation, dies when the tab closes. Buys you the persistence you
    asked for with a natural expiry that costs no code. Costs: a second tab starts logged out, and closing the tab loses
    it.
  - [1mPer-browser ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m)[0m — survives restarts and shared across tabs. Buys "still logged in tomorrow." Costs: on a
    shared or public machine the last person's userId is sitting there for the next one, and with no logout in the app
    there is no way to clear it. I'd want a logout in scope if we go here, which grows the work.
  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — a module variable, gone on reload. Cheapest and safest, but it doesn't persist, so it fails what
    you asked for. Listing it only so the tradeoff is visible.

  Both storage options are readable by any script on the origin, so neither should ever hold a credential — this is an
  identifier only, and the spec will say so.

  [1mMy recommendation: per-tab [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m.[0m It matches "persist" as most people mean it, and its expiry is automatic —
  which matters a lot given the app has no logout to hang clearing off. If you want per-browser later, that's a one-line
  storage swap [3mplus[0m a logout, and I'd rather add both deliberately than inherit a userId that never expires.
